<p>Cuba rejected allegations in an Axios report citing classified U.S. intelligence that said Havana had acquired more than 300 military drones and discussed possible strikes on the U.S. naval base at Guantanamo Bay, U.S. military vessels and Key West, Florida. Axios said U.S. officials believe Cuba has obtained attack drones with varying capabilities from Russia and Iran since 2023 and has placed them at strategic sites on the island. Cuban Foreign Minister Bruno Rodriguez accused Washington of building a “fraudulent case” to justify sanctions and possible military action, and said Cuba would defend itself against external aggression under the U.N. Charter. A senior U.S. official told Axios the Trump administration sees the issue as a growing threat because of advances in drone warfare and the reported presence of Iranian military advisers in Havana. The report followed a rare visit to Cuba by CIA Director John Ratcliffe, who met Cuban officials and warned against hostile actions, according to accounts citing a CIA source. Reuters could not independently verify the Axios report, according to a dispatch carried by Efsyn.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/EwaKyU2QKP8s8F88fABoHdKSQDuvnv6U-WGzMudXaD504UOKzWZ5udFVoQ4f3ObQBBx9BZ9dKmeXfHRez76so2wXc8Mn8ZVOP76t8yrHPTXq2oYtkJ1cu-riJJHHjPPOzIi5VYHBDyILtvIUyN6tUaRr_wB6ODtfSolzrhPpEKBGPOYsiUM_HGcWiruF3g9YpPKVcn3xFiAbjRrlH1fZaxJzqq7dp4fwGmk3AYonPio8GVFEVihe1dir_Wi1H64NaTHKTIBZXg' alt='People cook with firewood during a blackout in Havana, prompted by US President Donald Trump&#x27;s fuel blockade of Cuba.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Domestic preparations: Some reports said Cuban authorities were instructing the public about a possible “imperialist” or military invasion as tensions rose.</li><li>More equipment: Axios reported that Cuban officials sought additional drones and military equipment from Russia within the past month.</li><li>Legal move: U.S. media accounts said the Justice Department was expected this wee